hls: an interrupt in a segment's body reads on from where it stopped

When the caller's interrupt callback cuts the read of a segment's body
short, avio_read() of the segment's input returns AVERROR_EXIT and
read_playlist_data() took that as the segment's end: it closed the input,
moved to the next segment and requested it. A player that interrupts a
read for something other than a seek (a control request it handles and
then reads on) lost the rest of that segment: a silent gap of up to a
segment in sound and picture. 0076 and 0077 cover an interrupted request
and the buffer's error; nothing covered the body.

Return AVERROR_EXIT and keep the input, its offset in the segment and
the connection: http stops at an interrupt without closing the
connection, and an interrupted reconnect wait leaves http to reconnect at
its offset on the next read. The input's EOF flag and error, which
avio_read() set, are cleared, so the next call of the read callback reads
on from the same place. A seek closes the input as before.

diff --git a/libavformat/hls.c b/libavformat/hls.c
--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -2224,6 +2224,15 @@ restart:
             memmove(buf, buf + vtt_gap, ret);
         }
     }
+    if (ret == AVERROR_EXIT) {
+        /* The caller's interrupt callback cut the read of the segment's body
+         * short. An interrupt is transient (a seek starts reading anew
+         * anyway): keep the input and the offset, and read on from there on
+         * the next call, instead of dropping the rest of the segment. */
+        v->input->eof_reached = 0;
+        v->input->error = 0;
+        return ret;
+    }
     if (ret > 0) {
         if (just_opened && v->is_id3_timestamped != 0) {
             /* Intercept ID3 tags here, elementary audio streams are required
